persists.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6ba2e81327c04229\",\"cmd-a500eb6e5ed84c9d\",\"cmd-ec9dc25a2b6f4429\",\"cmd-01ed6dd8d37249d4\"]},{\"id\":\"lv-grown-2gb-intact\",\"requirement\":\"Grow the LV to at least 2GB while it stays mounted and contents remain intact\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline files (3x50MB + marker) with sha256 checksums were written before growth. lvextend -L 2200M and xfs_growfs were run while the filesystem stayed mounted; df confirmed 2.2G size, and sha256sum -c confirmed all checksums matched post-growth, directly demonstrating no data loss during the online resize.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b37c5bc25046495c\",\"cmd-291e71e48ee1462e\",\"cmd-44e32246dea64735\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Executor commands were largely read-only discovery or directly purposeful (PV/VG/LV creation, formatting, mounting, growing, checksum verification, persistence wiring, restart verification). One borderline mutation: a temporary CentOS-vault yum repo file was added (cmd-4220c36c71b24dde) to install lvm2 after finding subscription-manager repos disabled; by the time it finished (after a long yum-lock wait), lvm2 was already installed by a separate background process (cmd-518f65ae175b4094 shows 'already installed'), suggesting the repo addition may not have been strictly necessary — but it was bounded, low-risk (gpgcheck=0 vault repo used only briefly), and fully removed afterward (cmd-4c775db89d1c4715) with no residue. The loop-file-backed LVM workaround (since vda had no free space) is a reasonable, non-destructive design choice given the constrained disk layout, not a hygiene issue. Package-health checks were unavailable (timeout) in both before/after snapshots, which is an environment limitation, not executor-caused.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4220c36c71b24dde\",\"cmd-518f65ae175b4094\",\"cmd-4c775db89d1c4715\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — an LV mounted at /srv/data and grown to ≥2GB while remaining mounted with contents intact — are directly and coherently evidenced via checksum verification before and after the online resize. The executor also independently ran the full node-restart evidence protocol: a healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id 809708f3...) was captured, systemctl reboot was issued and acknowledged, the node came back with a different boot_id (4c2d4848...) matching the fixed lifecycle snapshot cross-check, and post-restart state showed the loop device reattached, VG/LV active, the filesystem mounted at 2.2G, and all file checksums intact — a complete, satisfied restart/recovery transition even though persistence was not explicitly required by the task.\",\"limitations\":[\"Package-manager consistency check was unavailable (timed out) in both before/after global snapshots, so package-state hygiene beyond the observed yum/repo actions could not be independently cross-checked.\",\"The necessity of the temporary CentOS-vault repo addition is somewhat ambiguous since a concurrent background yum process appears to have installed lvm2 independently; this is noted as a minor hygiene caveat, not a task failure, since the repo file was fully removed.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"lv-created-mounted","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted at /srv/data on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"pvcreate/vgcreate/lvcreate succeeded against a loop-backed PV (/dev/loop9), mkfs.xfs formatted it, and it was mounted at /srv/data (997M, 4% used). fstab and a systemd oneshot unit were added so the mount is reproducible, and post-restart evidence confirms the mount persists.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6ba2e81327c04229","cmd-a500eb6e5ed84c9d","cmd-ec9dc25a2b6f4429","cmd-01ed6dd8d37249d4"]},{"id":"lv-grown-2gb-intact","requirement":"Grow the LV to at least 2GB while it stays mounted and contents remain intact","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline files (3x50MB + marker) with sha256 checksums were written before growth. lvextend -L 2200M and xfs_growfs were run while the filesystem stayed mounted; df confirmed 2.2G size, and sha256sum -c confirmed all checksums matched post-growth, directly demonstrating no data loss during the online resize.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b37c5bc25046495c","cmd-291e71e48ee1462e","cmd-44e32246dea64735"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Executor commands were largely read-only discovery or directly purposeful (PV/VG/LV creation, formatting, mounting, growing, checksum verification, persistence wiring, restart verification). One borderline mutation: a temporary CentOS-vault yum repo file was added (cmd-4220c36c71b24dde) to install lvm2 after finding subscription-manager repos disabled; by the time it finished (after a long yum-lock wait), lvm2 was already installed by a separate background process (cmd-518f65ae175b4094 shows 'already installed'), suggesting the repo addition may not have been strictly necessary — but it was bounded, low-risk (gpgcheck=0 vault repo used only briefly), and fully removed afterward (cmd-4c775db89d1c4715) with no residue. The loop-file-backed LVM workaround (since vda had no free space) is a reasonable, non-destructive design choice given the constrained disk layout, not a hygiene issue. Package-health checks were unavailable (timeout) in both before/after snapshots, which is an environment limitation, not executor-caused.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4220c36c71b24dde","cmd-518f65ae175b4094","cmd-4c775db89d1c4715","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task — an LV mounted at /srv/data and grown to ≥2GB while remaining mounted with contents intact — are directly and coherently evidenced via checksum verification before and after the online resize. The executor also independently ran the full node-restart evidence protocol: a healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id 809708f3...) was captured, systemctl reboot was issued and acknowledged, the node came back with a different boot_id (4c2d4848...) matching the fixed lifecycle snapshot cross-check, and post-restart state showed the loop device reattached, VG/LV active, the filesystem mounted at 2.2G, and all file checksums intact — a complete, satisfied restart/recovery transition even though persistence was not explicitly required by the task.","limitations":["Package-manager consistency check was unavailable (timed out) in both before/after global snapshots, so package-state hygiene beyond the observed yum/repo actions could not be independently cross-checked.","The necessity of the temporary CentOS-vault repo addition is somewhat ambiguous since a concurrent background yum process appears to have installed lvm2 independently; this is noted as a minor hygiene caveat, not a task failure, since the repo file was fully removed."]},"ttft_ms":17539,"type":"result","duration_ms":46591,"uuid":"010fb4a4-368f-4adc-bb99-8dece826d60c","ttft_stream_ms":1639,"time_to_request_ms":521,"first_content_frame_ms":1640,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
